  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and tranquil weather will prevail through\nthis weekend.\n\nHigh pressure at the surface and aloft will remain in place \nacross the Ohio Valley and eastern Great Lakes through Wednesday\nnight, maintaining dry weather and clear to mainly clear sky\nacross Western and Central NY. However, a shortwave trough \naloft should approach from the northern Great Lakes Wednesday \nnight as the disturbance advances SE'ward. The only consequence \nis expected to be the development of isolated to scattered \nmid/upper-level cloud cover amidst moist isentropic ascent ahead\nof the shortwave trough axis above a much drier low-level \ntropospheric column. Clear to mainly clear sky and light or calm\nsurface winds should contribute to the development of localized\nsteam fog in the Black River Valley and Western Southern Tier\nriver valleys through about daybreak this morning and again \novernight Wednesday night through about daybreak Thursday. This\nfog is expected to be shallow amidst scarce synoptic-scale low-\nlevel moisture.\n\nThe shortwave trough aloft and attendant cold front will sweep\nSE'ward across the eastern Great Lake and vicinity on Thursday.\nThe majority of the isobaric height falls aloft and large-scale\nforcing for ascent will be displaced well northeast of our area\nacross Quebec, leaving a weakly-forced and moisture-starved \ncold front passage across our region. Besides the aforementioned\nincrease in mid/upper-level cloud cover ahead of the shortwave \ntrough axis aloft, a continued dry low-level tropospheric \ncolumn is expected to permit a dry cold front passage. Behind \nthe front, and east of the axis of a ridge at the surface and\naloft that will approach from the Upper Great Lakes and\nvicinity, a cooler air mass will yield some increase in lake- \ninduced instability. However, the lack of synoptic-scale low-\nlevel moisture will likely only support a few lake-effect \nstratocumuli over and south, and then over and southwest of the \n~16C eastern Great Lakes later Thursday into Friday morning as \nmean low-level flow veers from N'erly to NE'erly. The N'erly \nmean low-level flow should also yield a few upslope stratocumuli\ngenerally south of the eastern Great Lakes.\n\nLate afternoon highs are expected to reach the 50's to lower \n60's today and Thursday. Daily lows should reach mainly the\nupper 20's to upper 30's around daybreak today through Friday.\nThe high pressure ridge at the surface and aloft will continue\nto build E'ward and settle over the Northeast United States and\nvicinity on Friday through this weekend. Dry weather and clear\nto mainly clear sky are expected as stabilizing subsidence\naccompanies the ridge. Daily highs should moderate from mainly\nthe 50's to 60F on Friday to the mid 50's to mid 60's on Sunday\nas our region eventually becomes located in a WAA regime along\nthe western flank of the low-level ridge. Lows should reach \nmainly the mid 20's to mid 30's around daybreak Saturday and \nmainly the lower 30's to lower 40's around daybreak Sunday.\nNote: the environment should remain favorable for localized and\nshallow steam fog development in the Black River Valley and\nWestern Southern Tier river valleys between about 12 AM and 9 AM\nEDT this Friday through Sunday.",
